  "articleBody": "You’ve gathered a few roof estimates – that’s a great start! But the next step, reviewing and comparing them, is where many homeowners stumble. It’s tempting to just pick the lowest number, but that can lead to headaches, hidden costs, or a less-than-ideal roof down the road. Steering clear of these common mistakes will help you choose wisely.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n- Accepting a Verbal or Vague Estimate: A detailed estimate should be in writing and clearly itemize everything. If a contractor can't provide specifics on materials, labor, or disposal, that's a red flag. A vague estimate leaves too much open to interpretation and potential disputes.\n- Comparing Different Material Grades as Equivalent: Not all roofing materials are created equal. Don't assume 'shingles' from one company are the same as 'shingles' from another. Ask about manufacturers, product lines, and specific grades. A cheaper shingle might have a shorter lifespan or a weaker warranty.\n- Failing to Ask if Old Roofing is Removed: Some estimates might quote for an 'overlay' (installing new shingles over old ones) without explicitly stating it. Always confirm if a full tear-off is included. While an overlay can be cheaper upfront, it can hide underlying issues and isn't always the best long-term solution.\n- Ignoring Disposal and Permit Costs: These can add up! Make sure the estimate clearly states who is responsible for obtaining permits and how waste removal (dumpsters, debris cleanup) is handled and costed. Surprises here can significantly inflate your final bill.\n- Not Defining How Concealed Damage Will Be Handled: Once the old roof is removed, unexpected damage to the roof decking or sheathing can appear. A solid estimate will outline a clear process and pricing for these unforeseen repairs, so you're not caught off guard.\n- Focusing Only on the Headline Price: The lowest price isn't always the best value. Consider what's included, the quality of materials, the contractor's reputation (built on referrals, like Discounted Roofing Co.), and the completeness of the warranty. Investing a bit more upfront for quality can save you money and stress in the long run.\n\nBy avoiding these common pitfalls, you can make a more informed decision and ensure your new roof is exactly what you expect. We're here to provide transparent, honest advice so you can feel good about your choice.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start:\nGather all your written estimates and highlight any unclear sections or differences in scope to discuss during your follow-up questions.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}